<p>A horse-drawn wagon carrying a pioneer family attracted attention in 1877 because most settlers used oxen. The distinction proved fitting: Elijah Boley and his son {% person "alphonse-boley" %}Alphonso{% endperson %} became instrumental in developing regional horse racing, the Missouri Slope Fair, and Mandan's rodeo.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/elijah-boley/220-boley-portrait-gilbert-unconfirmed.jpg" alt="Unconfirmed portrait believed to show Elijah Boley">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Elijah Boley was born March 31, 1826, to Pennsylvania farmers {% person "john-boley" %}John{% endperson %} and {% person "mary-boley" %}Mary Boley{% endperson %}. He followed his sister {% person "mary-boley-beard" %}Mary{% endperson %} and brother-in-law {% person "john-beard" %}John Beard{% endperson %} to Mahoning County, Ohio, in the late 1840s and worked on their farm. Boley married {% person "sarah-boley" %}Sarah Lewallen{% endperson %} on December 31, 1853, in Indiana. The couple farmed, milled grain, and bought livestock in Bourbon Township until moving to Monona in Clayton County, Iowa, in 1863. There they operated a farm and small hardware business.</p>

<p>Elijah and Sarah had four children, two of whom survived to adulthood: Alphonso, born June 5, 1857, and {% person "viola-mae-boley-coe" %}Viola Mae{% endperson %}, born April 20, 1863. {% person "adolphus-boley" %}Adolphus{% endperson %} and {% person "alvaretta-boley" %}Alvaretta{% endperson %} died as children.</p>

<p>In 1877, Elijah and Alphonso claimed land at the present site of the Heskett Power Station north of Mandan, reportedly the fourth claim west of the Missouri River. Nearby Bellows Landing, later Rock Haven, served government steamboats. Boley expected the Northern Pacific bridge and an accompanying city to rise there, but surveyors selected a location farther south.</p>

<p>Sarah and Viola joined Elijah and Alphonso by train in early 1878. The family's holdings eventually reached 990 acres.</p>

<p>Boley became prominent in county and city affairs. He was appointed Morton County's first assessor in March 1878, helped organize election precincts and commission districts, served as an election judge, and offered his home as a polling place. Voters elected him to the first three-member county commission that November.</p>

<p>Boley also worked to secure Mandan as the site of the Reform School, now the Youth Correctional Center. In 1895, he deeded 40 acres for the institution and offered another 160 acres at $10 per acre, although the deed was later canceled after disagreements arose. The legislature approved the institution in 1902, and it opened at its present site in 1903.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/elijah-boley/199-1879-us-dept-of-interior-cushman-enlargement.jpg" alt="1879 map showing Morton County and surrounding Dakota Territory">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1879, the Dakota Territorial Legislature attached eastern Morton County to Burleigh County. Boley and {% person "frederic-francis-gerard" %}Frederic Gerard{% endperson %} led 26 citizens in petitioning Congress to restore Morton County's original boundaries. The 1881 territorial legislature ultimately did so. {% person "nehemiah-g-ordway" %}Governor Nehemiah Ordway{% endperson %} appointed Boley to the three-member commission that reorganized Morton County on February 25, 1881, and Boley became its chair.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/elijah-boley/320-morton-courthouse.jpg" alt="Morton County Courthouse on the Collins Avenue hill">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>When the county decided in 1885 to replace its courthouse—formerly a saloon and keno hall—Commission President Boley championed a site on the Collins Avenue hill. “In ten years Mandan will be as large as Chicago and the hill north of the city will be the center of population,” he proclaimed. The {% place "collins-street-county-courthouse" %}courthouse{% endplace %} opened the following year and burned in May 1941.</p>

<p>Beginning around 1880, Boley also selected seed from Arikara/Ree corn. Unlike farmers who sold the best ears, he reserved the best kernels for planting. His corn reportedly developed 12 rows per ear instead of the more common four to six. He said the variety matured rapidly and, because of its hardiness, was unlikely to fail if planted early enough.</p>

<p>Elijah and Sarah Boley remained near Mandan. Elijah died March 2, 1901, and is buried beside Sarah in Union Cemetery.</p>

